  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, pleasant weather through the end of the week as \nhigh pressure continues to influence our weather.\n \nSouthern New England will see temperatures about 10-12 degrees above \naverage today and Friday as a surge of warm air advection ahead of a \ncold front warms 925 mb temperatures to between +11 and +15C. After \nlows in the 30s and 40s this morning,  highs today quickly rebound \ninto the low to mid 60s, upper 60s for the Merrimack and Connecticut \nRiver Valleys. Highs climb a few degrees higher on Friday with many \nplaces warming near or just above 70 across the southern and eastern \ncoastal plains (mid 60s for the interior and Cape/Islands). As the \ncold front approaches Friday, winds are likely to be gustier than \nwe've seen the last several days...likely 20 to 25 mph at times, \npossibly 30 to 35 mph in the higher elevations.\n\nPost-FROPA Friday night, skies clear and winds calm for another \nnight of radiational cooling. Temperatures will once again drop into \nthe low to mid 30s for most.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure \nlikely to maintain dry weather through at least Monday. A slight\nrisk for some showers Tuesday into Wednesday, but the details \nare not yet certain.\n\nThe dominant high pressure should finally move off the coast\nSunday night into Monday. Even then, its influence is expected\nto slowly diminish into the middle of next week.\n\nThere are rather large discrepancies in the evolution of a mid \nlevel ridge across the central USA early next week. The\nmagnitude and orientation of this ridge will be the determining \nfactor for the track of a low pressure trying to move east from \nthe Rockies. at this time, thinking this low pressure will\nstruggle to move east quickly. That leaves the positioning of\nits associated fronts to consider. With the low pressure quite\na ways away from southern New England, there will not be much \npush to move them much as well.\n\nOverall, the most likely outcome is that dry weather prevails. \nHowever, a quasi-warm front may get close enough to our region \nTuesday into Wednesday to perhaps trigger a few showers. Not \nseeing much moisture to work with, so any precipitation would be \nlight.",
